            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest major news about NASA’s **Mars Perseverance rover** is:\n\n- **Perseverance has found evidence of ancient repeated asteroid impacts** in a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of layered rock on the rim of Jezero Crater, called the **“Broom Point member.”** NASA says these rocks are likely **more than 3.9 billion years old**, making them among the oldest terrain ever studied by a Mars rover. The findings, published July 15, 2026, suggest the area preserves a record from one of the early solar system’s most violent periods【5:1†source】.\n\n- The rover also recently hit a mobility milestone: it has driven the equivalent of a **full marathon — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km — on Mars**. It reached that mark on its **1,890th Martian day**, about five years and four months into the mission, far faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to cover the same distance【5:0†source】.\n\n- NASA’s Perseverance news feed also shows recent image releases from **Broom Point** and maps of the rover’s route there, highlighting its ongoing exploration beyond Jezero Crater’s rim【5:3†source】.",
